>
> Stan puts declarations of both data and parameters
> in one place -- the model file.  But data's stored
> separately.  And everything's typed.

What worked for me was that I'd think about, create, and compile the
model and then put it away.

Separately I'd think about, assemble, and point to the data.

Finally, I'd think about and create a simulation file that described the
experiments I wanted to run.

In most commercial system dynamics simulators, all three parts are
intertwined.  By separating them, I tended to think more deeply about
each...about the model, about the data, and about the experiments.  That
led me to factorial designs without thinking about it; it just felt
right to ask what sort of experiments I wanted to run, and a factorial
design seemed to be the answer.

>> In the hopes that these capabilities remain in the Stan ODE solver, here are a few other things I found useful.
>>

>> 1. the expressiveness of the language (I didn’t really miss the integral form)

>
