  z <- Integral(x+y, init=0)

>> 2. the ability to choose one’s solver

>
> We're hoping to just get one for stiff equations up before
> expanding our repertoire, but they'll definitely be plug-and-play
> like our samplers and optimizers if we do.

Ask Frederic; he's got Euler and at least one RK solver built in, and
deSolve adds more, I think.

>> 3. thanks to deSolve, the ability to handle events (although I have yet to use them)
>
> Not sure what this means.  We're going to have "dosing",
> which will allow arbitrary non-continuous shocks to be
> added to the system.

I imagine dosing and events are pretty much the same.

>> 4. The ability to handle tabular nonlinearities with arbitrary interpolation routines using the GSL or one’s own code

>
> I'll have to ask Frederic about this.

I may not have been clear.  In mathematical models, I may have a
variable that is a nonlinear function of another variable, and I'd right
x^2 or ln(y) or ....  In a system dynamics setting, we typically define
a table of (x,y) points that define a nonlinear function, and we select
an associated interpolation function.  Step interpolation is always
there, and some smoothed interpolation is, too.  I liked that MCSim let
me use the GSL to select from a broad range of library interpolation
functions.

