From ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com  Wed Sep 11 00:53:26 2013
From: ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com (Daniel Lee)
Date: Wed, 11 Sep 2013 00:53:26 +0000
Subject: Boost's uBLAS matrix library does not work with autodiff (example attached)
Message-ID: <I_XVKlb6fTQ.MeFFPIoPqkoJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/I_XVKlb6fTQ/m/MeFFPIoPqkoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In an earlier email, Bob outlined the problem:

> The issue with lifespan is that Boost's ublas library for matrices defines static class constants everywhere by wrapping the scalar type. And so does the rosenbrock functions.  This won't work with our variable lifespans. We'd need traits more like those in Eigen, which seem to be modeled on the function-based approach of std::numeric_limits.  I was sort of surprised to see Boost using static constants in ublas --- it's usually bad ju-ju in C++ because of initialization issues.  See:
>
>   /boost/numeric/ublas/matrix.hpp:
>   ----------------------------
>   ...
>   static const value_type one_;

The result of uBLAS's use of static constants is that we get a segmentation fault. The attached test, as written, does not seg fault. By accessing any element in the identity matrix (lines 9-12), it will seg fault.

No error messages, no nothing. It all happens before this code executes. It took us a while to figure out how and where the seg fault was coming from, but we found it.

In short, Stan's autodiff library will not work with Boost's uBLAS matrix library.

Daniel

P.S. This means Boost numeric's ODE solvers will not work with Stan. There are other issues to getting those ODE solvers to work.

From ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com  Wed Sep 11 01:41:11 2013
From: ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com (Daniel Lee)
Date: Wed, 11 Sep 2013 01:41:11 +0000
Subject: layout of tests (source files)
Message-ID: <yy_BSsgPHuE.vZVMx7Lu6WkJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/yy_BSsgPHuE/m/vZVMx7Lu6WkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I suggest we rearrange our test files into subdirectories like:
src/test/unit
src/test/distribution
src/test/model
...

Here are a couple reasons:

* The physical layout would clarify which tests belong to which makefile targets. Right now, some of the targets, like test-distributions, are in the src/test/ folder, but excluded by hand from the test-unit target.

* This will allow us to expand to Windows, Hotfoot, and Travis-CI specific tests. Our Windows toolchain is unable to handle the templating required for forward-mode auto-diff, so we need to find a way to exclude tests if we want to keep using our Jenkins box for continuous integration.

If anyone knows of a better or more standard way of organizing tests, please respond.

Daniel

