From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Sun Sep 15 18:00:13 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Sun, 15 Sep 2013 18:00:13 +0000
Subject: (R)Stan 2.0 schedule
Message-ID: <Ct4Njpf_7TE.PVusI-u4sw0J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/Ct4Njpf_7TE/m/PVusI-u4sw0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

EXEC SUMMARY:

   * Stan 2.0 beta testing in approximately two weeks.
   * Official Stan 2.0 version in approximately a month.
   * RStan 2.0 on roughly the same schedule

Longer version:

We froze features at our meeting early last week.

The triage on features left out of Stan 2.0 can be seen on
the issue tracker on GitHub by inspecting the milestones.

Late last week, Daniel and Michael managed to clear the
hurdle of integration testing for the new argument structure,
which is much more flexible and coherent and extensible than
what we had before.

All that's left to do now is

   * integrate the pileup of pull requests,
   * fix a couple (hopefully minor) bugs, and
   * update the docs with all the great comments we've gotten
     (keep them coming --- we do track them and pay attention)

We still have to coordinate with Jiqiang about RStan, and
my understanding is that the interfaces there won't change
as much as for Stan.   We've decoupled the projects' repositories,
and there's no reason for them to be completely synchronized
other than not to disappoint our R users (which is probably
most of our users at this point --- it's certainly how I use
Stan most of the time).

The next message on this topic will hopefully be beta-test
announcements (of course it'll be open --- everything in Stan's
open).

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Sun Sep 15 18:00:19 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Sun, 15 Sep 2013 18:00:19 +0000
Subject: ODE support in Stan: design docs and prototypes
Message-ID: <4oB6iECYUZc.E3Y7HEFkIikJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/4oB6iECYUZc/m/E3Y7HEFkIikJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We've been working on the design and building some early
prototypes for a new Stan feature that will let you define
systems of ordinary differential equations and build models
based on their solutions (typically measurement error models
to allow parameters of the system to be estimated from data).

The design thinking up to this point (or at least as much
of our discussions as I can remember) is now consolidated
on our Wiki:

https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/ODE-Integrator-Support

There's a link to the GitHub branch where Daniel and I are
building prototypes.

We've had lots of input already, especially from Andrew Gelman,
Michael Betancourt, Ben Goodrich and Frederic Bois (Frederic
built MCSim and works with Andrew on pharmacokinetic models).

We'd love to hear any other ideas people might have, so feel
free to comment here on our mailing lists or on the wiki itself.

- Bob

